## Breaking Changes

<!-- If breaking changes do *not* exist, add the following: -->
<!--
There are no breaking changes in AG Charts version {% migrationVersion() %}.
-->

<!-- Otherwise -->

The full list of breaking changes across all features for version {% migrationVersion() %}.

{% expandingSection headerText="Breaking Changes" %}

This release includes the following breaking changes:

### Number Axis Interval

- A number axis with `interval.step` no longer widens its domain to make room for its labels. The domain now follows the data and the step. For example, a bar chart with x values from `0` to `11000` and `interval: { step: 100 }` now has an x domain of `-500` to `11500`, where it was `-10000` to `20000`. To keep the previous domain, set `min` and `max`, for example `min: -10000, max: 20000`.
- `interval.step` rounds the domain out to a multiple of the step, even when the axis is short or `interval.minSpacing` is large, so the domain can differ from version 14. For example, `interval: { step: 25 }` on a chart 150 pixels high, with data up to `88.07`, now spans `0` to `100`, where it spanned `0` to `90`. To keep the previous domain, set `max`, for example `max: 90`.
- A `step` whose labels overlap is applied, where version 14 ignored a `step` that was too dense for a narrow chart. For example, `interval: { step: 100 }` in the same bar chart, 200 or 300 pixels wide, now shows 121 overlapping labels where version 14 ignored the `step` and showed 2. Use a larger `step` for the width of the chart, for example `step: 5000` at 300 pixels wide shows 5 labels without overlap. Alternatively, remove `step` and set `interval.minSpacing` so that the axis chooses the interval, for example `minSpacing: 50`.
- A value in `interval.values` that falls outside the axis domain is not shown. The domain now follows the data more closely, so a value that the wider domain in version 14 happened to cover can now fall outside it. For example, `interval: { values: [50, 88, 100] }` on data with a maximum of `88.07` now shows `50` and `88` on an axis from `0` to `90`, where it showed all three on an axis from `0` to `100`. A `values` array that only contains `100` shows no labels. A value far beyond the data, such as `500` in `[50, 88, 500]`, is not shown in either version. To show a value, extend the domain with `min`, `max`, `preferredMin` or `preferredMax`, for example `max: 100`. Setting `nice: false` does not bring the value back.
- With `interval.values`, the domain can sit closer to the data, so a data point can lie on the edge of the series area. For example, the x axis of the Bubble with Images gallery example, with `interval: { values: [-250, 250] }`, now spans `-5000` to `2000` and the "drums" bubble at `-5000` sits on its left edge, where the axis spanned `-6000` to `2000`. To keep the space, set `min` or `preferredMin`, for example `min: -6000`.

### Radius Axis Interval

- A radius axis with `interval.step` can have a different domain when the step is very small or `interval.minSpacing` is large. For example, `interval: { step: 30, minSpacing: 400 }` on data up to `92` now spans `0` to `120`, where it spanned `0` to `100`, and `interval: { step: 1 }` now spans `0` to `92`, where it spanned `0` to `100`. To keep the previous domain, set `max`, for example `max: 100`, which gives `0` to `100` with ticks at `0`, `30`, `60` and `90` for the first example, and `0` to `100` with a tick at every integer for the second.

### Gradient Legend Interval

- A gradient legend with `scale.interval.step` and a large `scale.interval.minSpacing` now places its ticks at multiples of the step, so its labels can differ. For example, a legend over values from `10` to `50` with `interval: { step: 30, minSpacing: 400 }` now shows `30`, where it showed `10` and `40`. Choose a `step` whose multiples fall where you want the labels, for example `step: 10` shows `10` to `50`, as it did in version 14.

{% /expandingSection %}

